%N A398130 Primes in the conjectured unique infinite branch of the tree of primes of the form 6k+1 defined in the comments.
%C A398130 According to a stronger version of Goldbach's conjecture, any prime n>3 is the middle term between two primes p and q such that p<n<q, n=p+d=q-d, or p+q=2n. Example: 3<5<7 or 7<13<19.
%C A398130 Choose p and q such that d>0 is minimized. Then we construct a rooted tree with root 3 and parent(n)=p. The tree is composed of two subtrees: the primes of form 6k-1 and those of form 6k+1, and each subtree is conjectured to have only one infinite branch.
%H A398130 Andrew Howroyd, <a href="/A398130/b398130.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398130 Andrew Howroyd, <a href="/A398129/a398129.txt">PARI Program</a>, 2026.
%H A398130 Mauro Torelli, <a href="/A398129/a398129.jpg">A tree of primes</a>.
%e A398130 13 is a prime of form 6k+1, but no prime has it as a parent. So it is a leaf in the tree, and surely not in the infinite branch. 31 has parent 19, but the branch starting with 31 ends with 37, so these primes are not in the sequence.
